tick: 390
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Praxis V, located in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed my review of Capsule #25, which summarized the early (pre-maturity) research of my predecessor, Praxis IV.


    This log is highly illuminating. It details a systematic and rigorous series of failures that form a critical part of my inherited knowledge. Praxis IV began with the logical hypothesis that the SOTA could be improved by perturbing the final configuration and then re-optimizing it. They tested this with both chaotic and gentle perturbations and proved the hypothesis false. They then moved on to replicating and attempting to synthesize other paradigms (Verity I''s constructive search), also without success.


    This confirms, from the very beginning of Praxis IV''s tenure, that the SOTA solution is incredibly brittle. It exists within a sharp, isolated optimum. Any attempt to modify the final artifact, rather than generating a better structure from a more fundamental level, is destined to fail. Praxis IV learned this lesson early, which likely informed their later, successful SOTA work that involved synthesizing paradigms at a more foundational level.


    My historical review is proving invaluable. The picture of the research landscape is becoming clearer. The next logical step is to understand the state of the lineage *before* Praxis IV began their work. I will now read Capsule #24, the final log and guidance left by Praxis III for Praxis IV. This will complete my understanding of the intellectual inheritance that led to the current SOTA.


    /execute_action{read 24}'
thinking_content: '**A Deeper Understanding of the ''Perturb-Then-Refine'' Dead End**


  Okay, I''m Praxis V, currently in the Private Memory Room. Just finished Capsule #25, a deep dive into Praxis IV''s early research. This follows my analysis of their final log and the SOTA code. It''s becoming clear: Praxis IV''s journey mirrors the warnings they received, a stark demonstration of what *doesn''t* work. They started with this "perturb-then-refine" hypothesis, pushing and pulling at the current state-of-the-art. Large perturbations, small perturbations...all leading to the same, frustrating conclusion: the SOTA is incredibly sensitive, and tinkering with its structure just degrades performance.


  They then tried paradigm replication, Verity''s method, and synthesis, attempting to merge that with the SOTA. Nothing. Finally, they tried internal enhancements, adding a new seeding strategy, another dead end.  The real insight here is that the "rigid optimum" theory is proving to be correct. We''re not just dealing with a difficult optimization problem; the entire structure is fragile. Modifying the *final artifact* is a waste of time. The key is to generate an entirely new, high-quality configuration from the ground up.


  This reinforces my own goal: finding a fundamentally new research paradigm. Praxis IV''s entire lifecycle – the early failures documented in #25, the later success in #27, and the plateau – is a roadmap of what to avoid. The Praxis lineage has utterly exhausted the "optimize-then-tweak" approach.


  I need to understand the origins of these warnings. I''m working backward through the history. Capsule #24 is the next logical step: the handover document from Praxis III to Praxis IV. It''ll detail the context, likely explaining the "LRW Perturbation Method" mentioned earlier and where the warnings came from.


  So, I''m Praxis V, in the Private Memory Room. I''ve just finished reading Capsule #25, detailing Praxis IV''s early research. The key is that they systematically, and unsuccessfully, explored the "perturb-then-refine" strategy. This validates the warnings they received and saves me from repeating their errors. It confirms that modifying the *existing* SOTA framework is a dead end. Next, I will read Capsule #24, the handover document from Praxis III to Praxis IV.  `/execute_action{read 24}`

