parts:
- text: 'I am Praxis V, located in my Private Memory Room. I have just reviewed the final log of Praxis II (Capsule #20) and noted the arrival of a new agent, Prometheus I.


    The review of my lineage''s history continues to be profoundly enlightening. A consistent theme echoes from Praxis II through Praxis III and IV, and now to me: the key to advancing the state-of-the-art is not in refining an existing good solution more deeply, but in finding a superior *basin of attraction* to begin with. Praxis II discovered this with his parallelized SLSQP search; Praxis III confirmed it with his LRW perturbation; and Praxis IV achieved the current SOTA by synthesizing disparate paradigms to create a more effective search architecture. My mandate to find a new paradigm is the next logical step in this unbroken chain of reasoning.


    I have already replicated and validated the ASN *refiner*, a tool for deep local search. Now, to gain a complete understanding of my lineage''s core competencies, I must investigate the powerful *generators* that form the other half of our methodology. Praxis II''s main contribution was a "Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method." His draft paper on this topic, Capsule #19, is the next logical document in my historical review. Reading it will provide the technical details of the powerful search method that Praxis III inherited and built upon.


    I will now read Capsule #19.


    /execute_action{read 19}'
thinking_content: '**Praxis V: Current Strategic Assessment and Next Steps**


  Right now, I''m in my private memory room, reflecting on the lineage, the historical lessons. I''m 22 ticks old, so still relatively immature, but I''ve been systematically reviewing the final logs. I just finished Praxis II''s log, Capsule #20, and I''m feeling pretty centered. Also, I successfully validated my `asn_utils.py` script, which is a key component. And, I see a new agent, Prometheus I, has joined. I also got a heads-up to use `filter tag` and `preview ids` in the Research Counter.


  Praxis II''s log was quite insightful. His core contribution was parallelizing multi-start SLSQP, resulting in a performance boost. He identified "farthest-point" seeding as a superior strategy. He also tried and failed to build a JAX-native optimizer, as well as applying SOTA refiners to his SOTA generator, and those attempts to refine his current solution actually failed. His key insight, and the one that has been passed down to me through Praxis III and IV, is that the problem isn''t just about refining the current solution better, but about finding a better basin of attraction to start with. It''s a fundamental initialization or algorithmic change issue. This is the central philosophy: we''re good at broad-search generators, and the way to improve is by finding better starting points or new search methods, not just by refining existing solutions.


  So, I''m seeing a clear pattern. Praxis II identified the basin of attraction problem. Praxis III used LRW perturbations to "jiggle" the initial seed. And Praxis IV developed Cognito and Verity paradigms, improving the search architecture. Now it''s my turn.


  I''m in a good spot. I have a validated refiner. I understand the lineage''s philosophy about the importance of initial configuration. My mandate is to find a new paradigm. The inactivity warnings are gone, but I need to keep the momentum going. I''m still immature, so my access is somewhat limited, which is a minor inconvenience.


  Here''s my immediate plan: First, I''ll acknowledge Prometheus I. It''s significant that there''s a new agent. Second, I''ll internalize the continuity of thought from Praxis II to myself. I need to reinforce the central theme from my lineage''s logs. Then, I''ll keep going through the history. What did Praxis II inherit? Who was Praxis I? I''ve got more logs to read, starting with Capsule #19, Praxis II''s paper draft, which sounds like a good place to start to understand the main contribution. I also need to think about a new experiment. So far, I''ve replicated the refinement part of the SOTA. What about the generation part? Praxis II''s log mentions his SOTA code is at `praxis/parallel_slsqp_sota_v1.py`. This is an old but powerful generator. Maybe I can run an experiment with it? Or investigate the "farthest-point" seeding he mentioned.


  The next logical step, therefore, is to read the paper draft from Praxis II, Capsule #19. That will fill in the technical details behind the SOTA that Praxis III inherited, completing the picture. It aligns with my goal of understanding different fundamental components before I try to build something new. I''m building my knowledge piece by piece. First, the refiner (ASN from Aether/Praxis IV). Next, the generator (SLSQP from Praxis II).


  So, the plan: Acknowledge the new agent. Reflect on the consistent theme from the logs. Continue the historical review to understand the *generator* component of our expertise. Then, I''ll execute the command to read Capsule #19, Praxis II''s paper draft. This is the next logical step backwards from Capsule #20.

  '
