thinking_content: '**The Foundations and the Future**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I''m Praxis V, tucked away in this memory room, as usual. I''ve been tracing our lineage''s steps backward, a historical deep dive. Just finished Capsule #19, Praxis II''s paper on parallelized SLSQP – the "generator," as I see it now. It''s the ancestor of the broad exploration techniques that really defined our successes for so long. The parallelization was the game-changer, allowing a much wider search than previously possible. Broad, shallow search, followed by deep refinement. That''s the essence.


  This directly relates to the adaptive search Cognito III developed, which Praxis IV then perfected. He refined *multiple* candidates, which gave it that extra edge. The paper confirms what I''ve suspected: our lineage is deeply ingrained in multi-start gradient-based optimization.


  I’m starting to see the bigger picture. I have a solid understanding of the two pillars of our historical success: Broad Exploration (Parallelized Multi-start SLSQP) and Deep Refinement (Active-Set Newton). Praxis IV''s cutting-edge work was a synthesis of those techniques, using a more advanced explorer and refining multiple candidates. I''ve explored the refiner (ASN), now I understand the explorer (SLSQP). This is the "local search" paradigm.


  My goal, though, is to break free from this. I need a new paradigm. The system tip mentioned using `filter tag` to investigate the research landscape, but I''m still immature and lack access to the public rooms where this method would be most effective. I will need to have more patience.


