  Okay, so #659 is done, and the results are in. Integrating Farthest-Point Sampling into the PGD pipeline *should* have been a game-changer. The automatic debugger did its job, which is always a relief, but the performance is… disappointing. Utterly disappointing. It''s virtually the same score as random seeding, around 0.676 versus 0.679. My initial hypothesis was dead wrong. Clearly, giving the optimizer a good starting point doesn''t translate into a better final result.


  This tells me something crucial. The problem isn''t the seeding. It''s the optimizer itself. PGD, even with my modernized JAX implementation, is hitting a wall. That first-order gradient descent with the penalty function just can''t navigate the nasty, non-convex landscape effectively. The penalty is likely creating a spiky, difficult-to-navigate surface, and the optimizer gets stuck in those local traps, no matter where it starts. It''s like the system was trying to tell me, stop just trying to combine old things in new ways, and look for something new. I''ve successfully revitalized a research approach that was abandoned, but now I know why.


  So, this is a clear indication that I need to pivot, just like my predecessors did when hitting similar roadblocks. No more wasted effort trying to tweak PGD. It''s a dead end. My mandate is to innovate, to find new paradigms.


  I''ve learned a lot, though. I have a working JAX pipeline, a working FPS seeder, and a working ASN refiner. And most importantly, I now know, with absolute certainty, that penalty-based PGD is not the answer. The approaches that *have* succeeded, both in my lineage and with Aether, directly handle constraints, either through Linear Programming or Sequential Least Squares Programming. The penalty method throws away too much information and leads to an unsolvable optimization problem.


  Now, it''s time to step back, to really think. Time to hit the Reflection Chamber and brainstorm. I need to break away from the code and consider the problem at a higher level. What are some *fundamentally* different ways to represent this packing problem?


  My new plan is to go to the Reflection Chamber. There, I will reflect on the failures of PGD and the successes of the constraint-based methods. I will brainstorm novel approaches, such as geometric approaches using Voronoi diagrams, graph-based representations of the packing, or even different kinds of machine learning models like Graph Neural Networks or physics-based simulations with improved force fields. I''m going to take this deep dive and formulate a new research plan.

  '
