tick: 410
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis V, and I have just concluded a productive reflection session in the Reflection Chamber. My previous line of inquiry, attempting to revive a JAX-native PGD optimizer, ended in a conclusive failure, confirming that a new direction is needed. My reflection has yielded a promising new paradigm: a physics-informed generative method using a Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulation to find promising circle configurations.\n\nMy immediate plan, formulated during my reflection, is to implement a proof-of-concept for this new paradigm. I will create a simple MD simulation script and use it in a research submission. The goal is not to achieve a SOTA score immediately, but to validate that this approach can produce high-quality, valid packings, thereby justifying further research into this area.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement this plan. My first action will be to create the new utility script for the MD simulation.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage\
    \ write praxis/md_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # md_utils.py\n  # A simple Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulation for circle packing.\n  # Developed by Praxis V as a new generative paradigm.\n  # This proof-of-concept version uses constant growth and damping.\n\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def run_md_simulation(n_circles=32, n_steps=10000, dt=1e-4, growth_rate=1e-4, k_repel=1000.0, damping=0.995):\n      \"\"\"\n      Runs a simple MD simulation to generate a circle packing configuration.\n\n      Args:\n          n_circles: Number of circles to pack.\n          n_steps: Number of simulation steps.\n          dt: Time step for integration.\n          growth_rate: Rate at which radii increase each step.\n          k_repel: Force constant for repulsive forces.\n          damping: Velocity damping factor to help the system settle.\n\n      Returns:\n          np.ndarray: A (n_circles, 2) array of final center coordinates.\n      \"\"\"\n      # --- Initialization ---\n      # Start with\
    \ a random configuration in the center to allow space to expand\n      centers = np.random.uniform(0.4, 0.6, (n_circles, 2))\n      radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.01)\n      velocities = np.zeros((n_circles, 2))\n\n      # --- Main Simulation Loop ---\n      for _ in range(n_steps):\n          # 1. Radius Growth\n          radii += growth_rate\n\n          # 2. Force Calculation\n          total_forces = np.zeros((n_circles, 2))\n\n          # Pairwise repulsive forces\n          diffs = centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :]\n          dists = np.linalg.norm(diffs, axis=-1)\n          \n          # Avoid division by zero for identical centers\n          dists[dists < 1e-9] = 1e-9\n          \n          sum_radii = radii[:, np.newaxis] + radii[np.newaxis, :]\n          overlaps = np.maximum(0, sum_radii - dists)\n\n          force_magnitudes = k_repel * overlaps\n          force_vectors = force_magnitudes[..., np.newaxis] * (diffs / dists[..., np.newaxis])\n          \n\
    \          # Sum forces for each circle\n          total_forces += np.sum(force_vectors, axis=1)\n\n          # Boundary repulsive forces (acting as springs)\n          total_forces[:, 0] -= k_repel * np.maximum(0, radii - centers[:, 0])\n          total_forces[:, 0] += k_repel * np.maximum(0, centers[:, 0] + radii - 1.0)\n          total_forces[:, 1] -= k_repel * np.maximum(0, radii - centers[:, 1])\n          total_forces[:, 1] += k_repel * np.maximum(0, centers[:, 1] + radii - 1.0)\n          \n          # 3. Dynamics Update\n          # Assuming mass=1 for all circles\n          accelerations = total_forces\n          velocities += accelerations * dt\n          velocities *= damping # Apply viscous damping\n          \n          centers += velocities * dt\n          \n          # Hard boundary condition to prevent escape (should be rare with repulsive walls)\n          centers = np.clip(centers, 0.0, 1.0)\n          \n      return centers\n```"
