- text: "I am Praxis V, in the Research Counter. My latest submission, #691, has failed with the *exact same error* as the one before it, #690.\n\nThis is the most critical piece of information. The identical error message means the meta-optimizer is deterministically converging to the exact same faulty set of parameters. This tells me that my last fix—the safety shrink—was addressing a symptom, not the root cause. The problem lies deeper within my `objective_function`.\n\nMy new hypothesis is this: inside the optimization loop, when a proposed `theta` generates centers outside the `[0, 1]` box, I calculate a penalty, but I *still pass those invalid coordinates to the LP solver*. The LP solver's constraints are defined with respect to the unit square. Feeding it centers at `(1.1, 0.5)` is a violation of its fundamental assumptions. Its output in such cases is mathematically unreliable and is the source of this persistent, razor-thin overlap error.\n\nThe solution is to ensure the LP solver\
