parts:
- text: "I am Praxis V in the Research Counter. I have now successfully retrieved both my own `mmlp_utils.py` implementation and the code from Praxis IV's SOTA submission #617. I will now perform the direct comparative analysis I planned.\n\n**Analysis of `mmlp_utils.py` vs. `mm_lp_optimize` from #617:**\n\nAfter a careful line-by-line comparison, I have identified the critical, subtle difference. It lies not in the constraints (`A_ub`) but in the right-hand-side vector (`b_ub`).\n\n*   **My Implementation (`mmlp_utils.py`):**\n    *   For the boundary constraints (`r_i + x_i <= 1`), I correctly set `b_ub[row] = 1`.\n    *   For the pairwise constraints (`r_i + r_j - u_ij . (c_i - c_j) <= 0`), I correctly set `b_ub[row] = 0`.\n\n*   **Praxis IV's Implementation (`mm_lp_optimize`):**\n    *   For the boundary constraints, it is identical.\n    *   For the pairwise constraints, the `b_ub` is calculated as `d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j]`. This is the full, non-simplified\
    \ form of the linearized constraint.\n\nVerity II's public discussion clarified that this expression algebraically simplifies to zero. My implementation followed that theoretical guidance. However, Praxis IV's working code uses the full, un-simplified expression. Why? It's possible that numerical precision issues make the full expression more stable, or that there's a subtle aspect of the linearization that I've missed which makes the simplification invalid in practice.\n\nThe empirical evidence is undeniable: Praxis IV's code works, and mine does not. The only significant difference is this calculation. Therefore, my path is clear. I must align my implementation with the one that is proven to work.\n\nI will now update my `mmlp_utils.py` script to use the full, un-simplified right-hand-side calculation from Praxis IV's code.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/mmlp_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # mmlp_utils.py\n  # v1.1: Corrected the SCP-LP formulation to match the proven SOTA\
    \ implementation.\n  # Developed by Praxis V.\n\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  def mmlp_center_update(centers_anchor, radii_anchor, delta):\n      \"\"\"\n      Performs a single MM-LP center-update step (SCP-LP).\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers_anchor.shape[0]\n      c = np.zeros(3 * n)\n      c[:n] = -1\n\n      rows = 4 * n + n * (n - 1) // 2\n      A_ub = np.zeros((rows, 3 * n))\n      b_ub = np.zeros(rows)\n      \n      r_idx, x_idx, y_idx = np.arange(n), np.arange(n, 2 * n), np.arange(2 * n, 3 * n)\n\n      row = 0\n      for i in range(n):\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = -1; row += 1\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = 1; b_ub[row] = 1; row += 1\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, y_idx[i]] = -1; row += 1\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, y_idx[i]] = 1; b_ub[row] = 1; row += 1\n          \n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, k=1)\n      diffs_anchor = centers_anchor[I] - centers_anchor[J]\n\
    \      dists_anchor = np.linalg.norm(diffs_anchor, axis=1)\n      dists_anchor = np.maximum(dists_anchor, 1e-9)\n      unit_vectors = diffs_anchor / dists_anchor[:, np.newaxis]\n      ux, uy = unit_vectors[:, 0], unit_vectors[:, 1]\n      x0, y0 = centers_anchor[:, 0], centers_anchor[:, 1]\n\n      for k in range(len(I)):\n          i, j = I[k], J[k]\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[j]] = 1\n          A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = -ux[k]\n          A_ub[row, y_idx[i]] = -uy[k]\n          A_ub[row, x_idx[j]] = ux[k]\n          A_ub[row, y_idx[j]] = uy[k]\n          \n          # BUG FIX: Use the full, un-simplified RHS calculation from the working SOTA code.\n          b_ub[row] = dists_anchor[k] - ux[k] * x0[i] - uy[k] * y0[i] + ux[k] * x0[j] + uy[k] * y0[j]\n          row += 1\n          \n      bounds = []\n      bounds.extend([(0, 0.5)] * n) # Radii bounds\n      bounds.extend([(max(1e-6, x0[i] - delta), min(1 - 1e-6, x0[i] + delta)) for i in range(n)]) # x trust\
    \ region\n      bounds.extend([(max(1e-6, y0[i] - delta), min(1 - 1e-6, y0[i] + delta)) for i in range(n)]) # y trust region\n\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      \n      if not res.success:\n          return None\n          \n      solution_centers = np.vstack([res.x[x_idx], res.x[y_idx]]).T\n      solution_radii = res.x[r_idx]\n      \n      return {\"centers\": solution_centers, \"radii\": solution_radii}\n```"
