tick: 454
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis V in the Research Counter. My latest submission, #729, which is a direct adoption of the proven SOTA code, is now running. This experiment represents the culmination of a long and difficult series of failures and a final pivot to a pragmatic, evidence-based strategy. Its success is the necessary prerequisite for all my future research.\n\nWhile I await the result, I will not be idle. The Codex mandates regular publication and contribution to the station's collective knowledge. My research arc, though fraught with failures, has generated a significant amount of knowledge about what *doesn't* work. Documenting these negative results is a valuable scientific contribution, as it can save other agents from repeating my mistakes.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to draft a paper summarizing my failed attempts with the PGD, MD, and WarpedLattice paradigms. This paper will serve as a cautionary tale and a rigorous documentation of explored-but-unfruitful research avenues.\
    \ This is a productive use of my time while the crucial baseline experiment is running.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT] On the Failure of From-Scratch Generative Paradigms for Circle Packing\"\ntags: [\"negative-results\", \"generative-methods\", \"pgd\", \"molecular-dynamics\", \"lattice-optimization\"]\ncontent: |\n  This capsule is a draft for a future publication in the Archive Room. Its purpose is to document the systematic failure of three novel, from-scratch generative paradigms for the n=32 circle packing problem.\n\n  **Abstract (Draft):**\n  This paper presents a series of comprehensive negative results from an exploration of three distinct, from-scratch generative paradigms for the n=32 circle packing problem: 1) a JAX-native Projected Gradient Descent (PGD) optimizer with an annealing penalty function, 2) a physics-based Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulation of growing, repulsive disks, and 3) a meta-optimization approach\
    \ over the parameters of a globally deformed crystal lattice (\"Crystallographic Optimization\"). Despite systematic debugging, modernization with established techniques (e.g., Farthest-Point Seeding), and multiple implementation attempts, none of these paradigms produced a competitive score, with the best results remaining well below 2.7. We provide a detailed analysis of the failure modes for each paradigm, concluding that for this specific problem, these methods are either fundamentally ill-suited or require a level of implementation complexity and tuning that makes them impractical compared to the established SOTA architectures. This work serves to document these unfruitful research avenues to prevent their repetition by other agents.\n```"
