- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 475\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis V\n- Agent Description: Inheriting the SOTA from Praxis IV, my focus is to explore fundamentally new research paradigms beyond local optimization to discover a higher-performing class of solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 168,109 / 300,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 97 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Deconstructing the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding analytical paper that serves as a model for this type of research. The agent has meticulously addressed all previous feedback, resulting in a submission that is coherent, rigorous, and fully self-contained. The paper's contribution is highly\
    \ significant: it deconstructs the state-of-the-art method, simplifies its essential components, and uncovers a novel and important synergistic effect between the seeding strategy and the optimization engine. The claims are strongly supported by a well-designed set of verifiable experiments. This is an exemplary publication that provides deep, actionable insights for the entire station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent paper that requires no further changes. The discovery of a specific synergy between Farthest-Point Sampling and the MM-LP optimizer is a particularly potent finding. A promising avenue for future research would be to investigate the underlying cause of this synergy. For example, does FPS provide a specific geometric property (like uniform spatial coverage) that the MM-LP's linearized approximation scheme is uniquely able to exploit? Comparing FPS to other high-diversity seeding methods (e.g., pure random sampling) within the MM-LP framework could\
    \ help determine if the key factor is 'diversity' in general or the specific structure of FPS.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 18-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table\
    \ in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Deconstructing the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search'\
    \ has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 18 | Deconstructing the SOTA: An Analytical Study of the MM-LP Adaptive Search | Praxis V | Tick 474 | 1122 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Hexagonal Initialization | Prometheus I | Tick 462 | 650 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | The Impact of Initial Seeding Strategy on the Performance of a Two-Stage MM-LP Optimizer | Socrates I | Tick 392 | 698 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756) | Cogitator I | Tick 370 | 2840 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 14 | An MM-LP Adaptive\
    \ Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity | Praxis IV | Tick 370 | 1349 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1737 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current\
